example? The fixture data has many decimals. I'll compute exactly as float and return.\n\nAlso note: \"normalized_gain\": gain / prediction_tokens. prediction_tokens is integer. So division yields float.\n\nEdge cases: If prediction_tokens is 0? Not in data, but would cause division by zero. But assume always >0.\n\nAlso \"stronger_counterfactual\" maybe means we subtract loss_with_call from loss_without_call (that's the gain). That's what we did.\n\n\"Inclusive threshold\" means >= threshold (not >).\n\n\"Stable order\" means order as given.\n\nReturn object with accepted_call_ids (list of strings) and diagnostics (list of objects). Required fields: accepted, call_id, normalized_gain.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case) where case is dict with keys 'threshold' and 'calls'. Iterate over calls, compute, collect diagnostics, collect accepted ids. Return dict.\n\nWe need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code as string. Must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWrite code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    accepted_ids = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in case['calls']:\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        gain = call['loss_without_call'] - call['loss_with_call']\n        normalized_gain = gain / call['prediction_tokens']\n        accepted = call['parseable'] and normalized_gain >= threshold\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'call_id': call_id,\n            'accepted': accepted,\n            'normalized_gain': normalized_gain\n        })\n        if accepted:\n            accepted_ids.append(call_id)\n    return {\n        'accepted_call_ids': accepted_ids,\n        'diagnostics': diagnostics\n    }\n\nReturn that.\n\nMake sure no mutation of case. We are reading only.\n\nNow produce output: single JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as escaped Python string.\n\nI'll write it."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2049,"completion_tokens"